  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 355 PM Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Strong to severe storms remain possible across \ncentral NC into mid evening. Mostly dry through tonight, although \npatchy light rain is possible toward morning, along with widespread \nlow clouds.\n\nStrong to severe storms are ongoing at this hour, with a general \nmotion to the SE and SSE. As these feed on moderate SBCAPE within an \nenvironment of favorable effective bulk shear of 30-45 kts and high \nDCAPE of 1000-1300 J/kg, isolated to scattered discrete cells and \nclusters producing 60+ mph winds and 1-2\" diameter hail remain \nlikely. A Severe Thunderstorm Watch was issued for all of central NC \nuntil 8 PM EDT, although the severe threat in far NW areas has \ndiminished as of now. \n\nAs these storms and its accompanying frontal zone settle into our \nsouthern areas by mid evening and to our S by midnight, more stable \nair and lingering low level moisture will combine to result in a \ngood chance for low clouds and areas of fog over much of the area \novernight into Sun morning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Today is the last dangerously hot day of this \nstretch, with a brief respite in the heat coming Sun through Tue. \nBut above normal temperatures return for Wed through Sat, with Thu \nas the hottest day.\n\nAs the aforementioned storms drop SE and SSE through the area, the \nrain-cooled air will bring a welcomed end to this stretch of \ndangerous heat. A cooler and less humid air mass building in from \nthe N and NE will bring near normal or even slightly below normal \ntemps for Sun-Tue, as broad but deep mid level troughing over the \nEastern Seaboard gradually lifts ENE off the coast. By mid week, mid \nlevel ridging will build back in from the W, from the mid Miss \nValley, pushing low level thicknesses back above normal. This heat \ndoesn't look quite as intense as this most recent heat wave, as the \ndewpoints should be a little lower, keeping peak heat index values \neven on Thu in the mid 90s to around 100F. Apart from a chance of \nlight rain Sun, dry weather is expected through at least mid week. \nRenewed mid level troughing digging through the Great Lakes region, \nOhio Valley and into the Appalachians late in the week should help \nknock temps back down closer to normal by Fri/Sat with increasing \nclouds and a return to shower/storm chances.",
